{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^### (4.4|6.5|7.2|7.4)|canonical|defn.*segment|encode|plain-data'\"' docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:45:(defn- plain-data?\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:54:                 (map? x) (and (every? plain-data? (keys x))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:55:                               (every? plain-data? (vals x)))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:56:                 (coll? x) (every? plain-data? x)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:58:           (plain-data? (meta x)))))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:63:  (when-not (plain-data? v)\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:65:   canonical byte encoding lands (docs/design/dao.jing.md, Canonical\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:66:   encoding). It is NOT that canonical encoding.\"\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:105:          ;; lists and seqs are one canonical value: = calls them equal and\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:111:(defn- canonical-print\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:121:                     (str \"^\" (canonical-print (meta n)) \" \" body)\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:127:                                       (str (canonical-print k) \" \"\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:128:                                            (canonical-print v)))\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:133:            (str \"#{\" (str/join \" \" (map canonical-print n)) \"}\"))\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:136:            (str \"[\" (str/join \" \" (map canonical-print n)) \"]\"))\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:139:            (str \"(\" (str/join \" \" (map canonical-print n)) \")\"))\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:144:  \"Transitional encoder: canonical-print over the order-normalized form,\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:146:   canonical byte encoding; see order-normalize.\"\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:148:  (canonical-print (order-normalize v)))\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:199:             (convert/utf8.encode s))\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:283:   Transitional: order-normalized pr-str is the current encoder, NOT the\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:284:   pinned cross-platform canonical byte encoding the spec calls for\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:287:   implementations sharing this exact print rule; when the canonical\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:294:(defn segment-key\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:305:(defn segment-address?\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:318:(defn segment-hash\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:6:2026-09-15: the map AST is the Universal AST's canonical representation that gets evaluated by the ast-walker (a LISP using maps, not a LISP using vectors instead of lists). However, the Universal AST is converted into flat per-node tuple rows (`[id tag & slots]`) that act like bytecode, preserving everything in the Universal AST so that the semantic VM can execute it quickly because of the linearization of the AST into tuples (the VM lowers these rows into an instruction vector for execution). These rows are what is content-hashed, stored, streamed, and named — there is no nested tuple tree (ruling 1 of 2026-09-14,\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:23:(UCF: the canonical instruction vector §7.3.2, contract stamp §7.3.3,\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:27:its open canonical encoding), and\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:162:consumer's map is its own value, though conceptually they all represent the canonical Universal AST (the semantic representation).\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:164:The **canonical stored artifact** is the **flat per-node row projection** of the map\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:194:*canonical* map AST (post-strip and post-saturate, with frontend `:yang/*` keys and stray `tail?` marks gone), and `rows → map → rows` is the identity on the rows.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:201:canonical value, and nothing runtime-`assoc`ed is ever projected back to\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:226:|         | `linearize/plain-data?` defines  |     