{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"agent_message","text":"## Findings\n\n1. **Blocking | [v2.cljc:576](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:576) | Lambda parameter kind disagrees with §2.3.**  \n   The authoritative table specifies `[:params :syms]`; the implementation declares `[:params :data]`. All 18 tag names, slot orders, and arities otherwise match. The special-case `mapv` does not enforce a vector of symbols. A correctly hashed `[:lambda nil child-id]` reconstructs successfully, then re-projects with `[]`, changing its address.  \n   **Fix:** Declare `:syms`, implement its conversion explicitly, and validate vector-of-symbols input.\n\n2. **Blocking | [v2.cljc:643](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:643), [jing.cljc:140](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:140) | Structural sharing silently overwrites retained metadata.**  \n   I reproduced this with two literal values:\n   ```clojure\n   (with-meta 'x {:meaning 1})\n   (with-meta 'x {:meaning 2})\n   ```\n   Used as an `:if`’s consequent and alternate, they receive the same address. Reconstruction returns `{:meaning 2}` metadata for **both** occurrences. The encoder’s scalar printing omits symbol metadata, and Clojure `=` ignores metadata, so the collision guard does not throw.\n\n   This concerns retained plain-data metadata under §2.2, not excluded metadata on variable names. The existing `=` assertions also miss the corruption.  \n   **Fix:** Preserve retained symbol metadata in the encoder and use a metadata- and type-sensitive comparison for the collision guard. Add explicit metadata assertions. The concurrent encoder fixes have not eliminated this failure.\n\n3. **Blocking | [v2.cljc:632](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:632) | Reader provenance can remain inside a row body.**  \n   Projecting a literal containing `(with-meta 'x {:line 77 :column 9})` leaves that metadata on the symbol in the emitted row; I verified this directly. Therefore the claim that source positions and reader metadata never enter row bodies is not exhaustive.\n\n   Node-level `:eid`, `:macro?`, `:phase-policy`, `:yang/*`, source fields, and non-application tail marks are correctly excluded. Conversely, ordinary literal maps containing those keyword names are data and should remain unchanged.  \n   **Fix:** Strip recognized reader provenance metadata inside payloads while preserving semantic plain-data metadata. Add nested payload cases to exclusion tests.\n\n4. **Blocking | [v2.cljc:667](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:667), [v2.cljc:718](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:718) | Accepted malformed rows can change silently on re-projection.**  \n   Verified examples:\n   - A correctly hashed application with `:tail? :not-a-bool` reconstructs unchanged, then projects as `:tail? true`.\n   - A correctly hashed lambda with nil params reconstructs, then projects with empty params.\n   - Adding an unreachable literal row succeeds; reconstruction and re-projection silently discard it.\n\n   The reported omissions are substantially accurate. However, the current design calls the lexical rule **`:variable-scope`**, not `:variable-bounds`. Hashing before slot validation can also produce encoder exceptions without the advertised `:rule`/`:id` defect structure.\n\n   A separate full §7.4 validator is a reasonable architectural boundary. These functions still need either enforced validated-input preconditions or sufficient rejection checks to avoid accepting and changing invalid representations.  \n   **Fix:** Validate slot kinds and root closure before reconstruction, preferably through the shared validator. Full path reporting, lexical-scope validation, and explicit cycle checking can remain a clearly tracked separate unit.\n\n5. **Non-blocking | [v2_test.cljc:171](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yin/vm_test.cljc:171) | Coverage is useful but insufficient to establish the claimed fidelity.**  \n   The corpus contains **28**, not 27, ASTs and genuinely covers all 18 tags. It exercises distinct lambda/application arities, both tail booleans, nested control flow, store-key types, and a nested stream chain. Coverage is thinner for resume, store-put, and individual stream operations.\n\n   The tag test compares the corpus against the implementation’s own dictionary, so it cannot detect dictionary drift. Both round-trip directions start from implementation-generated rows, and `=` cannot detect metadata loss. The standalone `(local 'x)` fixture also violates §7.4’s scope rule if treated as a complete valid program.  \n   **Fix:** Add independently specified grammar expectations, hand-authored row fixtures, metadata-sensitive checks, and the malformed cases above.\n\n## Canonicalization assessment\n\nExplicit `:tail? false` and `:operands []` are **correct** under §§2.1, 2.4, and 7.2. The identity law applies after stripping and saturation; raw `yang/compile` output need not already have that shape.\n\nFor valid boolean and vector slots, `boolean` and `mapv` do not merge distinct canonical values. Their problematic behavior is accepting invalid values and silently normalizing them beyond the specified defaults.\n\n## Verification and verdict\n\nI independently reproduced **15 tests / 127 assertions / 0 failures**, using Java with the cached classpath to avoid Clojure CLI cache writes. The additional probes reproduced the failures above. No files were edited; CLJD was not run.\n\n**Verdict: No.** The implementation is not fully faithful to the §2 grammar or the lossless round-trip contract and is not ready for Architect sign-off."}}
